== link:bazel-modules[Bazel Modules]

== Dune

The most widely-used build system for OCaml is link:https://dune.build/[Dune,window=_blank].  Here are some points of comparison:

* Dune can discover dependencies at build time. For example, if you
  want to compile `+a.mli+` and `+a.ml+`, you do not need to
  explicitly tell Dune that the latter depends on the former; it can
  figure that out at build time. In fact, you don't even have to list
  the files you want to build; Dune will assume that you want to
  compile all the source files in the directory containing the
  `+dune+` file, so it will discover the build inputs at build time.
  By contrast, Bazel expects all dependencies to be explicitly listed
  as inputs to the build process. This is to ensure replicable,
  deterministic builds. (NB: a Bazel rule could be designed to
  discover dependency relations among explicitly listed input files at
  build time, but the rules in `+rules_ocaml+` do not do this. That's
  a possible future enhancement.)

* Dune supports named "build profiles". Bazel does not really have a
  concept like that, but it does support `--config` groups - named
  sets of command-line options that may serve a similar function. The
  difference is that Dune's build profiles are defined as part of the
  build program, and Bazel's config groups are not.

* Extensibility is the probably the starkest difference between Dune
  and Bazel. Bazel is designed for extensibility. Users can define
  their own custom rules to extend `+rules_ocaml+` with new
  primitives, and they can use Bazel's link:https://bazel.build/extending/macros[symbolic macros] to abstract over common
  build patterns.

* Integrated support for programming in multiple languages. Bazel
  rulesets have been implemented for many languages. Of particular
  interest to many OCaml users are
  link:https://github.com/bazelbuild/rules_cc[rules_cc] for integrated
  C/C++ support,
  link:https://github.com/bazelbuild/rules_rust[rules_rust],
  link:https://github.com/bazel-contrib/rules_go[rules_go],
  link:https://github.com/tweag/rules_haskell[rules_haskell],
  and the
  several rulesets supporting Javascript integration: see link:https://bazel.build/docs/bazel-and-javascript[Javascript and Bazel].

* Bazel has a sophisticated link:https://bazel.build/query/guide[query] facility that can tell you all sorts of interesting things about your project.

* Another stark difference between Bazel and Dune is Bazel's
  (relatively recent) integrated package managment system, usually
  refered to as `bzlmod`. Integrated package management for Dune is
  under development (I believe it is getting close to completion at
  the time of writing), but I'm not sure how it compares to `bzlmod`.

* The Bazel package management system includes a "module extension"
  facility that can be used to integrate external build and package
  management systems, such as npm or maven. For any OCaml build tool,
  support for opam dependencies is a fundamental requirement; a new
  tool, link:https://github.com/obazl/tools_opam[tools_opam] uses
  Bazel's module extension system to provide integrated opam support for
  `+rules_ocaml+`.

* Dune builds often include "rule" stanzas that are essentially little
  shell scripts. Bazel has a predefined rule (called `genrule`,
  general rule) that can be used in a similar manner. There are also
  several shell rulesets available that can be used for more complex
  cases.

* Remote caching and execution.

* Performance profiling.  Bazel supports several methods for link:https://bazel.build/advanced/performance/build-performance-metrics[profiling build performance].
